IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `hpnl-dates`

-- Health Practitioner Regulation National Law (Western Australia) -- the provisions a person who has been harmed by, or
-- has a concern about, a registered health practitioner meets when raising it with the regulator: who a notification can
-- be about (ss 138-139B), the grounds and who may notify (ss 144-145), how to notify and the help the National Agency
-- (Ahpra) must give (ss 146-147), mandatory notifications by practitioners, employers and education providers (ss 140-143,
-- with Western Australia's s 141 modifications), what happens to a notification (ss 149-152: the 60-day preliminary
-- assessment, the health complaints entity, no further action, telling the practitioner), what the notifier must or may
-- be told at each later stage (ss 159A, 161(3), 167A, 177A, 180, 187, 189, 192), professional indemnity insurance (ss 129, 130),
-- and the notifier's protections (s 237 liability, s 237A reprisals, s 237B non-disclosure agreements).
--
-- SEAT: the notifier -- usually a patient (or a patient's family member) harmed by, or worried about, a registered health
-- practitioner's care or conduct, who may also be bringing or settling a negligence claim. "Can I complain to Ahpra about
-- my surgeon? Will the surgeon be told? By when must the Board look at it? Can my settlement's confidentiality clause stop
-- me? Was the surgeon required to be insured?" Every judgment the Law gives a National Board, a panel or a court ("reasonably
-- believes", "is satisfied", "in good faith", "not in the public interest") is asked as an input, never decided.
--
-- Edition: the compilation on the Western Australian Legislation website (mrdoc_49311), retrieved 7 September 2026, which
-- includes the Queensland amendments of 2022 and 2025 that have commenced in Western Australia (the last commencing on
-- 10 April 2026), as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/mrdoc_49311.txt; with the Health Practitioner Regulation
-- National Law Application Act 2024 (WA) (mrdoc_49650) for s 19 (the State Administrative Tribunal) and the s 141
-- modifications (ss 27, 28). The approved registration standards on professional indemnity insurance are not deposited.
--
-- NOT ENCODED: see NOTES.md (Scope). In short: Parts 1-7 other than ss 129, 130 and the definitions the questions use;
-- Part 8 Divisions 7-14 other than the provisions about the notifier; Parts 9-12 other than ss 237-237B; the title and
-- practice protections (ss 113-123A) and interim prohibition orders (Div 7A) for unregistered persons.
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written from the notifier's seat with the 2026-10-18 defaults. No pipeline, no coverage table, no
-- independent test pass, no human gate. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Health Practitioner Regulation National Law (WA) -- the notifier`

§§ `Questions this encoding does not cover`

-- The stable l4 has no REFUSE. A question that can reach a gap answers with an `Answer`: either `answered` with the value,
-- or `not covered` with the reason.
DECLARE `Why not covered` IS ONE OF
    `Part 8 speaks of a person registered under this Law, or formerly registered, or registered under a corresponding prior Act; for a person never registered see the title and practice protections and interim prohibition orders (Part 7 Division 10, Part 8 Division 7A), not encoded`
    `section 139A reaches a former practitioner's behaviour only while the person was registered; this Law does not say what may be done about behaviour after registration ended`
    `section 144 does not list the matter you describe as a ground; the National Board decides in its preliminary assessment whether a notification relates to a ground (s 149(1)(b))`
    `this Law does not say; Division 2 of Part 8 puts the duty to notify only on registered health practitioners, employers and education providers`
    `section 237A commenced on 1 December 2025; earlier conduct falls under the Law as it stood before then, which is not deposited`

GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH A TYPE
DECLARE `Answer` a IS ONE OF
    `answered`    HAS `the answer` IS AN a
    `not covered` HAS `because`    IS A `Why not covered`

-- ======================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 138-139B -- can a notification be made about this person`

-- s 5: "registered health practitioner means an individual who -- (a) is registered under this Law to practise a health
-- profession, other than as a student; or (b) holds nonpractising registration under this Law in a health profession";
-- "student means a person whose name is entered in a student register as being currently registered under this Law".
-- A "corresponding prior Act" is the State or Territory registration Act this Law replaced (s 5; borrowed, asked).
DECLARE `The person's registration` IS ONE OF
    `registered now under this Law (general, specialist, provisional, limited or non-practising registration)`
    `registered now as a student`
    `registered under this Law before, but not now`
    `registered only under a corresponding prior Act (before this Law), never under this Law`
    `never registered as a health practitioner`

-- s 138(1): "occurs while the practitioner is registered ... (b) occurred before the practitioner was registered ...
-- (c) occurred during any other period in which the practitioner was not registered".
DECLARE `When the behaviour happened` IS ONE OF
    `while the person was registered`
    `before the person was first registered`
    `during a gap in registration (for example after it ended, lapsed, or was suspended or cancelled)`

DECLARE `Can a notification be made about them` IS ONE OF
    `yes: a notification may be made about a registered health practitioner for behaviour while registered (s 138(1)(a))`
    `yes: a notification may be made for behaviour before registration (s 138(1)(b)), but that behaviour cannot be unsatisfactory professional performance or unprofessional conduct (s 138(2))`
    `yes: a notification may be made for behaviour in a gap in registration (s 138(1)(c)); it cannot be unsatisfactory professional performance, nor unprofessional conduct except where s 139 applies (practising on after registration ended under s 108(2)(a)) (s 138(2))`
    `yes: a notification may be made about a registered student, on the grounds in s 144(2)`
    `yes: a notification may be made about a former practitioner for behaviour while registered, as if still registered (s 139A(2)); Part 8 applies except Divisions 2 and 6 (s 139A(3))`
    `yes: a notification may be made about a person registered under a corresponding prior Act for behaviour while so registered, to the extent one could have been made under that Act (s 139B(2), (3)); Part 8 applies except Divisions 2 and 7 (s 139B(4))`
    `no: for a person registered only under a corresponding prior Act, s 139B applies only to the extent a notification could have been made, and proceedings taken, under that Act (s 139B(3))`

@export Can a notification (complaint) be made to Ahpra under the Health Practitioner Regulation National Law (WA) about this health practitioner, given their registration and when the behaviour happened?
GIVEN `their registration` IS A `The person's registration` @desc The practitioner's registration status today, as the public register or the practitioner shows it
      `when it happened` IS A `When the behaviour happened` @desc When the behaviour you are concerned about happened, compared with the practitioner's registration
      `the prior Act allowed it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only for a person registered under a corresponding prior Act (a State or Territory registration Act before this Law): whether a complaint about the behaviour could have been made, and proceedings taken, under that Act
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Can a notification be made about them`
DECIDE `Can a notification be made about this person question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `their registration` EQUALS `registered now as a student`
            THEN `answered` `yes: a notification may be made about a registered student, on the grounds in s 144(2)`
        IF `their registration` EQUALS `never registered as a health practitioner`
            THEN `not covered` `Part 8 speaks of a person registered under this Law, or formerly registered, or registered under a corresponding prior Act; for a person never registered see the title and practice protections and interim prohibition orders (Part 7 Division 10, Part 8 Division 7A), not encoded`
        IF `their registration` EQUALS `registered now under this Law (general, specialist, provisional, limited or non-practising registration)`
            THEN `the s 138 answer` `when it happened`
        IF NOT (`when it happened` EQUALS `while the person was registered`)
            THEN `not covered` `section 139A reaches a former practitioner's behaviour only while the person was registered; this Law does not say what may be done about behaviour after registration ended`
        IF `their registration` EQUALS `registered under this Law before, but not now`
            THEN `answered` `yes: a notification may be made about a former practitioner for behaviour while registered, as if still registered (s 139A(2)); Part 8 applies except Divisions 2 and 6 (s 139A(3))`
        IF `the prior Act allowed it`
            THEN `answered` `yes: a notification may be made about a person registered under a corresponding prior Act for behaviour while so registered, to the extent one could have been made under that Act (s 139B(2), (3)); Part 8 applies except Divisions 2 and 7 (s 139B(4))`
        OTHERWISE `answered` `no: for a person registered only under a corresponding prior Act, s 139B applies only to the extent a notification could have been made, and proceedings taken, under that Act (s 139B(3))`

-- s 138(1), (2).
GIVEN w IS A `When the behaviour happened`
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Can a notification be made about them`
`the s 138 answer` w MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF w EQUALS `while the person was registered`
            THEN `answered` `yes: a notification may be made about a registered health practitioner for behaviour while registered (s 138(1)(a))`
        IF w EQUALS `before the person was first registered`
            THEN `answered` `yes: a notification may be made for behaviour before registration (s 138(1)(b)), but that behaviour cannot be unsatisfactory professional performance or unprofessional conduct (s 138(2))`
        OTHERWISE `answered` `yes: a notification may be made for behaviour in a gap in registration (s 138(1)(c)); it cannot be unsatisfactory professional performance, nor unprofessional conduct except where s 139 applies (practising on after registration ended under s 108(2)(a)) (s 138(2))`

-- ======================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 144-145 -- can I make a voluntary notification`

-- s 145: "Any entity that believes that a ground on which a voluntary notification may be made exists in relation to a
-- registered health practitioner or a student may notify the National Agency." s 5: "entity includes a person and an
-- unincorporated body". The grounds are s 144(1) (practitioner) and s 144(2) (student).
DECLARE `Who the notification is about` IS ONE OF
    `a registered health practitioner`
    `a registered student`

DECLARE `Can I notify` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you may notify the National Agency, because you believe a ground in s 144(1) exists (ss 144(1), 145)`
    `yes: you may notify the National Agency about the student, because you believe a ground in s 144(2) exists (ss 144(2), 145)`

-- s 144(2)(a): "the student has been charged with an offence, or has been convicted or found guilty of an offence, that
-- is punishable by 12 months imprisonment or more".
GIVEN `charged or convicted` IS A BOOLEAN, `maximum months` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the s 144(2)(a) ground holds` `charged or convicted` `maximum months` IF
    `charged or convicted` AND `maximum months` AT LEAST 12

@export Can I make a voluntary notification (complaint) about a registered health practitioner or student to Ahpra under the Health Practitioner Regulation National Law (WA)?
GIVEN `about` IS A `Who the notification is about` @desc Whether your concern is about a registered health practitioner or about a registered student
      `conduct` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Practitioner: you believe their professional conduct is, or may be, below what the public or their professional peers might reasonably expect (s 144(1)(a))
      `care` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Practitioner: you believe the knowledge, skill or judgment they have, or the care they took, is or may be below the standard reasonably expected (s 144(1)(b))
      `unsuitable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Practitioner: you believe they are not, or may not be, a suitable (fit and proper) person to be registered (s 144(1)(c))
      `impairment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Practitioner or student: you believe they have, or may have, a physical or mental impairment, disability, condition or disorder (including substance abuse) that affects, or is likely to affect, their capacity to practise or train (s 144(1)(d), (2)(b))
      `contravened the Law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Practitioner: you believe they have, or may have, broken the National Law (for example practising without professional indemnity insurance) (s 144(1)(e))
      `contravened a condition` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Practitioner or student: you believe they have, or may have, broken a condition of their registration or an undertaking given to their Board (s 144(1)(f), (2)(c))
      `improperly registered` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Practitioner: you believe their registration was, or may have been, obtained with false or misleading information or documents (s 144(1)(g))
      `charged or convicted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Student: the student has been charged with, convicted of, or found guilty of an offence
      `maximum months` IS A NUMBER @desc Student: the longest prison term, in months, that offence can be punished by (0 if none)
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Can I notify`
DECIDE `Can I make a voluntary notification question` IS
    IF `about` EQUALS `a registered health practitioner`
    THEN IF    `conduct` OR `care` OR `unsuitable` OR `impairment`
            OR `contravened the Law` OR `contravened a condition` OR `improperly registered`
         THEN `answered` `yes: you may notify the National Agency, because you believe a ground in s 144(1) exists (ss 144(1), 145)`
         ELSE `not covered` `section 144 does not list the matter you describe as a ground; the National Board decides in its preliminary assessment whether a notification relates to a ground (s 149(1)(b))`
    ELSE IF    `the s 144(2)(a) ground holds` `charged or convicted` `maximum months`
            OR `impairment`
            OR `contravened a condition`
         THEN `answered` `yes: you may notify the National Agency about the student, because you believe a ground in s 144(2) exists (ss 144(2), 145)`
         ELSE `not covered` `section 144 does not list the matter you describe as a ground; the National Board decides in its preliminary assessment whether a notification relates to a ground (s 149(1)(b))`

-- ======================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 146-147 -- how I notify, and the help I can get`

-- s 146(1): "A notification may be made to the National Agency -- (a) verbally, including by telephone; or (b) in writing,
-- including by email or other electronic means." (2): "must include particulars of the basis on which it is made."
-- (3): "If a notification is made verbally, the National Agency must make a record of the notification."
-- s 147(1): "The National Agency must, if asked by an entity, give the entity reasonable assistance to make a
-- notification". (2): "Without limiting subsection (1), the National Agency may assist an entity to make a notification
-- if -- (a) the entity is not able to put the entity's notification in writing without assistance; or (b) the entity
-- needs assistance to clarify the nature of the individual's notification."
DECLARE `Must Ahpra help me` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you asked, so the National Agency must give you reasonable assistance to make the notification (s 147(1))`
    `the National Agency may help you without being asked, because you cannot put it in writing without help or need help to clarify it (s 147(2)); if you ask, it must (s 147(1))`
    `not unless you ask: the National Agency must give reasonable assistance if asked (s 147(1))`

@export Must Ahpra help me make my notification under the Health Practitioner Regulation National Law (WA)?
GIVEN `I asked for help` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have asked the National Agency (Ahpra) to help you make the notification
      `I need help to write or clarify it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you cannot put the notification in writing without help, or need help to make clear what it is about
GIVETH A `Must Ahpra help me`
DECIDE `Must Ahpra help me make my notification question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `I asked for help`
            THEN `yes: you asked, so the National Agency must give you reasonable assistance to make the notification (s 147(1))`
        IF `I need help to write or clarify it`
            THEN `the National Agency may help you without being asked, because you cannot put it in writing without help or need help to clarify it (s 147(2)); if you ask, it must (s 147(1))`
        OTHERWISE `not unless you ask: the National Agency must give reasonable assistance if asked (s 147(1))`

DECLARE `How I notify` IS ONE OF
    `by telephone or in person`
    `in writing, by letter, email or online`

DECLARE `Is my notification properly made` IS ONE OF
    `yes: a notification may be made verbally, including by telephone, and the National Agency must record it (s 146(1)(a), (3))`
    `yes: a notification may be made in writing, including by email or other electronic means (s 146(1)(b))`
    `not yet: a notification must include particulars of the basis on which it is made (s 146(2))`

@export Is my notification to Ahpra properly made under the Health Practitioner Regulation National Law (WA), given how I make it and what it says?
GIVEN `how` IS A `How I notify` @desc Whether you notify by speaking to Ahpra (telephone or in person) or in writing (letter, email, online form)
      `it says why` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your notification gives the particulars of what happened and why you are concerned
GIVETH A `Is my notification properly made`
DECIDE `Is my notification properly made question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`it says why`)
            THEN `not yet: a notification must include particulars of the basis on which it is made (s 146(2))`
        IF `how` EQUALS `by telephone or in person`
            THEN `yes: a notification may be made verbally, including by telephone, and the National Agency must record it (s 146(1)(a), (3))`
        OTHERWISE `yes: a notification may be made in writing, including by email or other electronic means (s 146(1)(b))`

-- ======================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 140-143 -- was someone else required to notify (mandatory notifications)`
-- Arises only where a practitioner, an employer or an education provider knew of the conduct.

-- s 140: "notifiable conduct, in relation to a registered health practitioner, means -- (a) practising the practitioner's
-- profession while intoxicated by alcohol or drugs; or (b) engaging in sexual misconduct in connection with the practice
-- of the practitioner's profession; or (c) placing the public at risk of substantial harm in the practitioner's practice
-- of the profession because the practitioner has an impairment; or (d) placing the public at risk of harm by practising
-- the profession in a way that constitutes a significant departure from accepted professional standards."
DECLARE `What the conduct was` IS ONE OF
    `practising while intoxicated by alcohol or drugs`
    `sexual misconduct in connection with practising the profession`
    `an impairment placing the public at risk of substantial harm`
    `a significant departure from accepted professional standards placing the public at risk of harm`
    `something else (for example a poor outcome, rudeness, or a billing dispute)`

GIVEN c IS A `What the conduct was`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is notifiable conduct` c IF
    NOT (c EQUALS `something else (for example a poor outcome, rudeness, or a billing dispute)`)

DECLARE `Who knew` IS ONE OF
    `a registered health practitioner, in the course of practising their profession`
    `a registered health practitioner, outside the course of practising their profession`
    `the practitioner's employer (under a contract of employment or for services)`
    `the student's education provider`
    `someone else`

-- s 141(4) (with (ca) inserted by the Application Act 2024 (WA) s 27(2)): the first health practitioner "does not form
-- the reasonable belief in the course of practising the profession if -- (a) ... is employed or otherwise engaged by an
-- insurer that provides professional indemnity insurance that relates to the second health practitioner or student; and
-- ... forms the reasonable belief ... as a result of a disclosure made ... in the course of a legal proceeding or the
-- provision of legal advice arising from the insurance policy; or (b) ... in the course of providing advice ... for the
-- purposes of a legal proceeding or the preparation of legal advice; or (c) ... is a legal practitioner and forms the
-- reasonable belief in the course of providing legal services to the second health practitioner or student ...; or
-- (ca) ... in the course of providing health services to the second health practitioner or student; or (d) ... as a
-- member of a quality assurance committee ... and is unable to disclose the information ... because a provision of that
-- Act prohibits the disclosure ...; or (e) ... knows, or reasonably believes, the National Agency has been notified".
DECLARE `How the practitioner came to know` IS ONE OF
    `in ordinary practice (none of the exceptions below)`
    `working for the other practitioner's indemnity insurer, from a disclosure in a legal proceeding or legal advice under the policy`
    `giving advice for a legal proceeding or legal advice`
    `as a lawyer acting for the other practitioner or student`
    `while treating the other practitioner or student as their patient`
    `on a quality assurance committee whose Act forbids disclosing it`
    `knowing or reasonably believing Ahpra had already been notified`

DECLARE `Was a notification required` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the practitioner had to notify the National Agency as soon as practicable after forming the reasonable belief (s 141(2)); not doing so is not an offence but may lead to action under Part 8 (s 141(3))`
    `yes: the employer had to notify the National Agency (s 142(1)); if the Agency learns it did not, it must report the failure to the responsible Minister, who must report it to a health complaints entity, the employer's licensing authority or another appropriate entity (s 142(2), (3))`
    `yes: the education provider had to notify the National Agency (s 143(1)); not doing so is not an offence, but the National Board must publish details of the failure on its website (s 143(2), (3))`
    `no: the conduct is not notifiable conduct as s 140 defines it (a voluntary notification may still be made)`
    `no: the student's impairment is not one that may place the public at substantial risk of harm in clinical training (s 141(1)(b), s 143(1))`
    `no: a mandatory notification turns on a reasonable belief (ss 141(1), 142(1), 143(1))`
    `no: section 141 applies only to a belief formed in the course of practising the profession (s 141(1))`
    `no: a belief formed working for the other practitioner's indemnity insurer from a disclosure in a legal proceeding or legal advice is not formed in the course of practising (s 141(4)(a))`
    `no: a belief formed giving advice for a legal proceeding or legal advice is not formed in the course of practising (s 141(4)(b))`
    `no: a belief formed as a lawyer acting for the other practitioner or student is not formed in the course of practising (s 141(4)(c))`
    `no: in Western Australia a belief formed while treating the other practitioner or student is not formed in the course of practising (s 141(4)(ca), inserted by the Application Act 2024 (WA) s 27)`
    `no: a belief formed on a quality assurance committee whose Act forbids disclosure is not formed in the course of practising (s 141(4)(d))`
    `no: a practitioner who knows or reasonably believes the National Agency has already been notified does not form the belief in the course of practising (s 141(4)(e))`
    `no: the student's education provider must notify only if the student is enrolled in its program of study or it arranged the student's clinical training (s 143(1))`
    `no: section 142 places the employer's duty only for a registered health practitioner, not a student`

@export Was a health practitioner, employer or education provider required by the Health Practitioner Regulation National Law (WA) to notify Ahpra about what happened (a mandatory notification)?
GIVEN `about` IS A `Who the notification is about` @desc Whether the conduct is that of a registered health practitioner or a registered student
      `who knew` IS A `Who knew` @desc Who formed the belief about the conduct or impairment
      `what it was` IS A `What the conduct was` @desc For a practitioner: what the conduct was
      `student risk` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a student: whether the student has an impairment that, in clinical training, may place the public at substantial risk of harm
      `the belief was reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who knew formed a reasonable belief about it (for an employer or education provider: reasonably believed it)
      `how they knew` IS A `How the practitioner came to know` @desc For a practitioner who knew: how they came to know (choose ordinary practice unless one of the exceptions applies)
      `enrolled or placed by them` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For an education provider: whether the student is enrolled in its program of study, or it arranged the student's clinical training
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Was a notification required`
DECIDE `Was a notification required question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `who knew` EQUALS `someone else`
            THEN `not covered` `this Law does not say; Division 2 of Part 8 puts the duty to notify only on registered health practitioners, employers and education providers`
        IF `who knew` EQUALS `the practitioner's employer (under a contract of employment or for services)` AND `about` EQUALS `a registered student`
            THEN `answered` `no: section 142 places the employer's duty only for a registered health practitioner, not a student`
        IF `who knew` EQUALS `the student's education provider` AND `about` EQUALS `a registered health practitioner`
            THEN `not covered` `this Law does not say; Division 2 of Part 8 puts the duty to notify only on registered health practitioners, employers and education providers`
        IF `about` EQUALS `a registered health practitioner` AND NOT (`is notifiable conduct` `what it was`)
            THEN `answered` `no: the conduct is not notifiable conduct as s 140 defines it (a voluntary notification may still be made)`
        IF `about` EQUALS `a registered student` AND NOT (`student risk`)
            THEN `answered` `no: the student's impairment is not one that may place the public at substantial risk of harm in clinical training (s 141(1)(b), s 143(1))`
        IF NOT (`the belief was reasonable`)
            THEN `answered` `no: a mandatory notification turns on a reasonable belief (ss 141(1), 142(1), 143(1))`
        IF `who knew` EQUALS `the practitioner's employer (under a contract of employment or for services)`
            THEN `answered` `yes: the employer had to notify the National Agency (s 142(1)); if the Agency learns it did not, it must report the failure to the responsible Minister, who must report it to a health complaints entity, the employer's licensing authority or another appropriate entity (s 142(2), (3))`
        IF `who knew` EQUALS `the student's education provider`
            THEN `the s 143 answer` `enrolled or placed by them`
        IF `who knew` EQUALS `a registered health practitioner, outside the course of practising their profession`
            THEN `answered` `no: section 141 applies only to a belief formed in the course of practising the profession (s 141(1))`
        OTHERWISE `answered` (`the s 141(4) answer` `how they knew`)

GIVEN placed IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Was a notification required`
`the s 143 answer` placed MEANS
    IF placed
    THEN `answered` `yes: the education provider had to notify the National Agency (s 143(1)); not doing so is not an offence, but the National Board must publish details of the failure on its website (s 143(2), (3))`
    ELSE `answered` `no: the student's education provider must notify only if the student is enrolled in its program of study or it arranged the student's clinical training (s 143(1))`

GIVEN h IS A `How the practitioner came to know`
GIVETH A `Was a notification required`
`the s 141(4) answer` h MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF h EQUALS `working for the other practitioner's indemnity insurer, from a disclosure in a legal proceeding or legal advice under the policy`
            THEN `no: a belief formed working for the other practitioner's indemnity insurer from a disclosure in a legal proceeding or legal advice is not formed in the course of practising (s 141(4)(a))`
        IF h EQUALS `giving advice for a legal proceeding or legal advice`
            THEN `no: a belief formed giving advice for a legal proceeding or legal advice is not formed in the course of practising (s 141(4)(b))`
        IF h EQUALS `as a lawyer acting for the other practitioner or student`
            THEN `no: a belief formed as a lawyer acting for the other practitioner or student is not formed in the course of practising (s 141(4)(c))`
        IF h EQUALS `while treating the other practitioner or student as their patient`
            THEN `no: in Western Australia a belief formed while treating the other practitioner or student is not formed in the course of practising (s 141(4)(ca), inserted by the Application Act 2024 (WA) s 27)`
        IF h EQUALS `on a quality assurance committee whose Act forbids disclosing it`
            THEN `no: a belief formed on a quality assurance committee whose Act forbids disclosure is not formed in the course of practising (s 141(4)(d))`
        IF h EQUALS `knowing or reasonably believing Ahpra had already been notified`
            THEN `no: a practitioner who knows or reasonably believes the National Agency has already been notified does not form the belief in the course of practising (s 141(4)(e))`
        OTHERWISE `yes: the practitioner had to notify the National Agency as soon as practicable after forming the reasonable belief (s 141(2)); not doing so is not an offence but may lead to action under Part 8 (s 141(3))`

-- ======================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 149-152 -- what happens to my notification`

-- s 149(1): "A National Board must, within 60 days after receipt of a notification, conduct a preliminary assessment of
-- the notification and decide -- (a) whether or not the notification relates to a person who is a health practitioner or
-- a student registered in a health profession for which the Board is established; and (b) whether or not the
-- notification relates to a matter that is a ground for notification; and (c) ... whether or not it is a notification
-- that could also be made to a health complaints entity." The period is reckoned by Schedule 7 cl 31(2), (3).
@export By when must the National Board finish its preliminary assessment of my notification under the Health Practitioner Regulation National Law (WA)?
GIVEN `the day the Board received it` IS A DATE @desc The date the National Board received your notification (ISO 8601, e.g. 2026-03-15)
GIVETH A DATE
DECIDE `By when must the Board assess my notification question` IS
    `the s 149 assessment deadline for a notification received on` `the day the Board received it`

@export Did the National Board finish its preliminary assessment of my notification within the 60 days the Health Practitioner Regulation National Law (WA) allows?
GIVEN `the day the Board received it` IS A DATE @desc The date the National Board received your notification (ISO 8601)
      `the day it decided` IS A DATE @desc The date the Board made its preliminary assessment decisions under s 149(1)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Was my notification assessed in time question` IF
    `the day it decided` `falls on or before` (`the s 149 assessment deadline for a notification received on` `the day the Board received it`)

-- s 150(1): "If the subject matter of a notification would also provide a ground for a complaint to a health complaints
-- entity ..., the National Board that received the notification must, as soon as practicable after its receipt -- (a)
-- notify the health complaints entity ...; and (b) give to the health complaints entity -- (i) a copy of the notification
-- ...; and (ii) any other information the Board has that is relevant". (2): the same the other way, for "a complaint about
-- a health practitioner" received by a health complaints entity. (3): "The National Board and the health complaints entity
-- must attempt to reach agreement about how the notification or complaint is to be dealt with". (4): "If [they] are not
-- able to reach agreement ..., the most serious action proposed by either must be taken."
-- s 5: "health complaints entity means an entity -- (a) that is established by or under an Act of a participating
-- jurisdiction; and (b) whose functions include conciliating, investigating and resolving complaints made against health
-- service providers and investigating failures in the health system". In Western Australia that is the Health and
-- Disability Services Complaints Office (borrowed; named in the @desc only).
DECLARE `Where I raised it` IS ONE OF
    `with Ahpra (a notification under this Law)`
    `with a health complaints entity (in Western Australia, the Health and Disability Services Complaints Office)`

DECLARE `Will the other body hear of it` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the National Board must, as soon as practicable, tell the health complaints entity and give it a copy and any other relevant information (s 150(1)); the two must try to agree how it is dealt with, and if they cannot, the most serious action either proposes must be taken (s 150(3), (4))`
    `yes: the health complaints entity must, as soon as practicable, tell the National Board and give it a copy and any other relevant information (s 150(2)); the two must try to agree how it is dealt with, and if they cannot, the most serious action either proposes must be taken (s 150(3), (4))`
    `no: section 150(1) applies only if the subject matter would also be a ground for a complaint to a health complaints entity`
    `no: section 150(2) applies to a complaint about a health practitioner (an individual who practises a health profession)`

@export If I raise my concern with Ahpra, or with the Health and Disability Services Complaints Office, will the other body be told under the Health Practitioner Regulation National Law (WA)?
GIVEN `where` IS A `Where I raised it` @desc Where you made your notification or complaint
      `it is also a complaint ground` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a notification to Ahpra: whether what you complain of would also be a ground for a complaint to a health complaints entity (in WA, HaDSCO), for example about the service you received
      `it is about a practitioner` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a complaint to a health complaints entity: whether it is about an individual who practises a health profession (rather than, say, only a hospital or a service)
GIVETH A `Will the other body hear of it`
DECIDE `Will the other body be told question` IS
    IF `where` EQUALS `with Ahpra (a notification under this Law)`
    THEN IF `it is also a complaint ground`
         THEN `yes: the National Board must, as soon as practicable, tell the health complaints entity and give it a copy and any other relevant information (s 150(1)); the two must try to agree how it is dealt with, and if they cannot, the most serious action either proposes must be taken (s 150(3), (4))`
         ELSE `no: section 150(1) applies only if the subject matter would also be a ground for a complaint to a health complaints entity`
    ELSE IF `it is about a practitioner`
         THEN `yes: the health complaints entity must, as soon as practicable, tell the National Board and give it a copy and any other relevant information (s 150(2)); the two must try to agree how it is dealt with, and if they cannot, the most serious action either proposes must be taken (s 150(3), (4))`
         ELSE `no: section 150(2) applies to a complaint about a health practitioner (an individual who practises a health profession)`

-- s 151(1): "A National Board may decide to take no further action in relation to a notification if -- (a) the Board
-- reasonably believes the notification is frivolous, vexatious, misconceived or lacking in substance; or (b) given the
-- amount of time that has elapsed since the matter the subject of the notification occurred, it is not practicable for
-- the Board to investigate or otherwise deal with the notification; or (c) the person to whom the notification relates
-- has not been, or is no longer, registered in a health profession for which the Board is established and it is not in
-- the public interest for the Board to investigate or otherwise deal with the notification; or (d) the subject matter of
-- the notification has already been dealt with adequately by the Board; or (e) the subject matter of the notification --
-- (i) is being dealt with, or has already been dealt with, by another entity; or (ii) has been referred by the Board
-- under section 150 or 150A to another entity to be dealt with by that entity; or (f) the health practitioner to whom the
-- notification relates has taken appropriate steps to remedy the matter ... and the Board reasonably believes no further
-- action is required". (2): no further action "does not prevent a National Board or adjudication body taking the
-- notification into consideration at a later time as part of a pattern of conduct or practice". (3), (4): the Board
-- "must give written notice of the decision to the notifier", stating the decision and "the reason".
DECLARE `Can the Board take no further action` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the Board may decide to take no further action (s 151(1)); if it does, it must tell you in writing, with its reason (s 151(3), (4)), and it may still take your notification into account later as part of a pattern (s 151(2))`
    `no: section 151 lets the Board take no further action only if one of its grounds holds (s 151(1)(a)-(f))`

@export Can the National Board decide to take no further action on my notification under the Health Practitioner Regulation National Law (WA), and must it tell me why?
GIVEN `frivolous` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Board reasonably believes your notification is frivolous, vexatious, misconceived or lacking in substance (s 151(1)(a))
      `too old` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, because of the time since it happened, it is not practicable for the Board to investigate or deal with it (s 151(1)(b))
      `not registered with this Board` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the practitioner has not been, or is no longer, registered in a profession for this Board (s 151(1)(c))
      `not in the public interest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a practitioner not (or no longer) registered with this Board: whether it is not in the public interest for the Board to deal with it (s 151(1)(c))
      `already dealt with by the Board` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Board has already dealt adequately with the same subject matter (s 151(1)(d))
      `dealt with or referred elsewhere` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether another body (for example the Health and Disability Services Complaints Office, a court or the police) is dealing or has dealt with it, or the Board referred it to another body under s 150 or s 150A (s 151(1)(e))
      `remedied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the practitioner has taken appropriate steps to remedy the matter (s 151(1)(f))
      `no more needed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a remedied matter: whether the Board reasonably believes no further action is required (s 151(1)(f))
GIVETH A `Can the Board take no further action`
DECIDE `Can the Board take no further action question` IS
    IF     `frivolous`
        OR `too old`
        OR (`not registered with this Board` AND `not in the public interest`)
        OR `already dealt with by the Board`
        OR `dealt with or referred elsewhere`
        OR (`remedied` AND `no more needed`)
    THEN `yes: the Board may decide to take no further action (s 151(1)); if it does, it must tell you in writing, with its reason (s 151(3), (4)), and it may still take your notification into account later as part of a pattern (s 151(2))`
    ELSE `no: section 151 lets the Board take no further action only if one of its grounds holds (s 151(1)(a)-(f))`

-- s 152(1): "A National Board must, as soon as practicable after receiving a notification about a registered health
-- practitioner or student, give written notice of the notification to the practitioner or student." (2): "The notice
-- must advise ... of the nature of the notification." (3): "Despite subsection (1), the National Board is not required to
-- give ... notice of the notification if the Board reasonably believes doing so would -- (a) prejudice an investigation of
-- the notification; or (b) place at risk a person's health or safety or place a person at risk of intimidation or
-- harassment."
DECLARE `Will the practitioner be told` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the National Board must, as soon as practicable, tell the practitioner or student in writing about the notification and its nature (s 152(1), (2))`
    `not necessarily: the Board is not required to tell the practitioner or student, because it reasonably believes doing so would prejudice an investigation or put a person at risk (s 152(3))`

@export Will the practitioner be told about my notification under the Health Practitioner Regulation National Law (WA)?
GIVEN `it would prejudice an investigation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Board reasonably believes telling the practitioner would prejudice an investigation of your notification
      `it would put someone at risk` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Board reasonably believes telling the practitioner would put a person's health or safety at risk, or put a person (for example you) at risk of intimidation or harassment
GIVETH A `Will the practitioner be told`
DECIDE `Will the practitioner be told question` IS
    IF `it would prejudice an investigation` OR `it would put someone at risk`
    THEN `not necessarily: the Board is not required to tell the practitioner or student, because it reasonably believes doing so would prejudice an investigation or put a person at risk (s 152(3))`
    ELSE `yes: the National Board must, as soon as practicable, tell the practitioner or student in writing about the notification and its nature (s 152(1), (2))`

-- ======================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 159A-192 -- what I am told later, and taking part in a panel hearing`
-- Arises only once the Board or a panel has decided something on the notification.

-- s 159A(2) (immediate action), s 167A(2) (decision after investigation), s 177A(2) (decision after an assessor's
-- report): the National Board "may inform the notifier who made the notification of the decision and the reasons for the
-- decision". s 180(1): "As soon as practicable after making a decision under section 179(2), the National Board must give
-- written notice of the decision to -- ... (b) if the decision was the result of a notification, the notifier." (2): "A
-- notice under subsection (1)(b) may also include the reasons". s 192(2): "The National Board must, within 30 days after
-- the panel makes its decision, give written notice of the decision to -- ... (b) if the hearing related to a
-- notification, the notifier." (4): "A notice under subsection (2)(b) may also include the reasons".
DECLARE `What was decided` IS ONE OF
    `the Board decided to investigate (s 160)`
    `the Board took immediate action (for example suspended the practitioner or imposed conditions) (s 156)`
    `the Board decided what to do after an investigation (s 167)`
    `the Board decided what to do after a health or performance assessment report (s 177)`
    `the Board took action after the show cause process (caution, undertaking, conditions, reprimand and the like) (s 179(2))`
    `a health panel or performance and professional standards panel decided the matter (s 191 or s 191A)`

DECLARE `What I must be told` IS ONE OF
    `the Board must give you written notice of the progress of the investigation at not less than 3 monthly intervals (s 161(3)(b)); the exceptions in s 161(4) cover only notices to the practitioner or student`
    `the Board may tell you of the decision and its reasons, but need not (s 159A(2))`
    `the Board may tell you of the decision and its reasons, but need not (s 167A(2))`
    `the Board may tell you of the decision and its reasons, but need not (s 177A(2))`
    `the Board must give you written notice of the decision as soon as practicable; it may also give the reasons (s 180(1)(b), (2))`
    `the Board must give you written notice of the panel's decision within 30 days after the panel makes it; it may also give the reasons (s 192(2)(b), (4))`

@export What must the National Board tell me, as the notifier, about a decision on my notification under the Health Practitioner Regulation National Law (WA)?
GIVEN `what was decided` IS A `What was decided` @desc The stage at which the Board or a panel decided something on your notification
GIVETH A `What I must be told`
DECIDE `What must I be told about the decision question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `what was decided` EQUALS `the Board decided to investigate (s 160)`
            THEN `the Board must give you written notice of the progress of the investigation at not less than 3 monthly intervals (s 161(3)(b)); the exceptions in s 161(4) cover only notices to the practitioner or student`
        IF `what was decided` EQUALS `the Board took immediate action (for example suspended the practitioner or imposed conditions) (s 156)`
            THEN `the Board may tell you of the decision and its reasons, but need not (s 159A(2))`
        IF `what was decided` EQUALS `the Board decided what to do after an investigation (s 167)`
            THEN `the Board may tell you of the decision and its reasons, but need not (s 167A(2))`
        IF `what was decided` EQUALS `the Board decided what to do after a health or performance assessment report (s 177)`
            THEN `the Board may tell you of the decision and its reasons, but need not (s 177A(2))`
        IF `what was decided` EQUALS `the Board took action after the show cause process (caution, undertaking, conditions, reprimand and the like) (s 179(2))`
            THEN `the Board must give you written notice of the decision as soon as practicable; it may also give the reasons (s 180(1)(b), (2))`
        OTHERWISE `the Board must give you written notice of the panel's decision within 30 days after the panel makes it; it may also give the reasons (s 192(2)(b), (4))`

-- s 161(3): "Also, the National Board must, at not less than 3 monthly intervals, give the written notice of the progress
-- of the investigation to -- (a) the registered health practitioner or student; and (b) if the investigation relates to
-- a notification made about the registered health practitioner or student, the notifier." (4): "However, the National
-- Board need not give the registered health practitioner or student a notice under subsection (1) or (3) if ...". Read
-- as "at least every 3 months" (see NOTES.md, finding 2).
@export By when must the National Board next tell me how its investigation of my notification is going, under the Health Practitioner Regulation National Law (WA)?
GIVEN `the day of the last notice` IS A DATE @desc The date the Board decided to investigate, or the date of its last progress notice to you if it has sent one (ISO 8601)
GIVETH A DATE
DECIDE `By when must I next hear of the investigation question` IS
    `the last day of 3 months after` `the day of the last notice`

@export By when must the National Board tell me of a panel's decision on my notification under the Health Practitioner Regulation National Law (WA)?
GIVEN `the day the panel decided` IS A DATE @desc The date the health panel or performance and professional standards panel made its decision (ISO 8601)
GIVETH A DATE
DECIDE `By when must I be told of the panel decision question` IS
    `the s 192 notice deadline for a panel decision on` `the day the panel decided`

-- s 187: "If a matter the subject of a hearing before a panel relates to a notification, the notifier may, with the leave
-- of the panel, make a submission to the panel about the matter." s 189: "A hearing before a panel is not open to the
-- public."
DECLARE `Can I make a submission to the panel` IS ONE OF
    `yes: as the notifier you may make a submission to the panel about the matter, with the panel's leave (s 187); the hearing is not open to the public (s 189)`
    `not without the panel's leave: a notifier may make a submission only with the leave of the panel (s 187)`
    `no: section 187 lets the notifier make a submission only where the matter relates to the notification`

@export Can I make a submission to the panel hearing about my notification under the Health Practitioner Regulation National Law (WA)?
GIVEN `the hearing is about my notification` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the matter before the panel relates to the notification you made
      `the panel gave me leave` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the panel has given you permission (leave) to make a submission
GIVETH A `Can I make a submission to the panel`
DECIDE `Can I make a submission to the panel question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`the hearing is about my notification`)
            THEN `no: section 187 lets the notifier make a submission only where the matter relates to the notification`
        IF `the panel gave me leave`
            THEN `yes: as the notifier you may make a submission to the panel about the matter, with the panel's leave (s 187); the hearing is not open to the public (s 189)`
        OTHERWISE `not without the panel's leave: a notifier may make a submission only with the leave of the panel (s 187)`

-- ======================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 129-130 -- did the practitioner who treated me have to be insured`

-- s 129(1): "A registered health practitioner must not practise the health profession in which the practitioner is
-- registered unless appropriate professional indemnity insurance arrangements are in force in relation to the
-- practitioner's practice of the profession." (4): "A contravention of subsection (1) ... does not constitute an offence
-- but may constitute behaviour for which health, conduct or performance action may be taken." (5): "registered health
-- practitioner does not include a registered health practitioner who holds nonpractising registration". s 5:
-- "appropriate professional indemnity insurance arrangements ... means professional indemnity insurance arrangements that
-- comply with an approved registration standard" (the standards are not deposited; whether arrangements comply is
-- asked as a fact).
DECLARE `Their registration type` IS ONE OF
    `practising registration (general, specialist, provisional or limited)`
    `non-practising registration`
    `student registration`
    `not registered`

DECLARE `Did they have to be insured` IS ONE OF
    `yes: they must not practise unless appropriate professional indemnity insurance arrangements are in force (s 129(1)); they had them`
    `yes, and they were not: practising without appropriate professional indemnity insurance arrangements breaches s 129(1); it is not an offence but may lead to health, conduct or performance action (s 129(4)), and you may notify on that ground (s 144(1)(e))`
    `no: s 129 does not apply while the practitioner is not practising the profession`
    `no: s 129 does not apply to non-practising registration (s 129(5)); a practitioner with non-practising registration must not practise at all (s 75)`
    `no: s 129 applies to a registered health practitioner, which by definition excludes registration as a student (s 5)`
    `no: s 129 applies only to a registered health practitioner (s 5)`

@export Did the practitioner who treated me have to have professional indemnity insurance under the Health Practitioner Regulation National Law (WA)?
GIVEN `their registration` IS A `Their registration type` @desc The practitioner's registration when they treated you, as the public register shows it
      `they were practising` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the practitioner was practising their health profession (for example treating you) at the time
      `they had compliant cover` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether professional indemnity insurance arrangements meeting their Board's registration standard were in force for their practice then (you may not know; answer as you believe)
GIVETH A `Did they have to be insured`
DECIDE `Did the practitioner have to be insured question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `their registration` EQUALS `not registered`
            THEN `no: s 129 applies only to a registered health practitioner (s 5)`
        IF `their registration` EQUALS `student registration`
            THEN `no: s 129 applies to a registered health practitioner, which by definition excludes registration as a student (s 5)`
        IF `their registration` EQUALS `non-practising registration`
            THEN `no: s 129 does not apply to non-practising registration (s 129(5)); a practitioner with non-practising registration must not practise at all (s 75)`
        IF NOT (`they were practising`)
            THEN `no: s 129 does not apply while the practitioner is not practising the profession`
        IF `they had compliant cover`
            THEN `yes: they must not practise unless appropriate professional indemnity insurance arrangements are in force (s 129(1)); they had them`
        OTHERWISE `yes, and they were not: practising without appropriate professional indemnity insurance arrangements breaches s 129(1); it is not an offence but may lead to health, conduct or performance action (s 129(4)), and you may notify on that ground (s 144(1)(e))`

-- s 130(1): "A registered health practitioner or student must, within 7 days after becoming aware that a relevant event
-- has occurred ..., give the National Board ... written notice of the event." s 130(3) relevant event (c): "appropriate
-- professional indemnity insurance arrangements are no longer in place in relation to the practitioner's practice of the
-- profession". (2): not an offence, but may be behaviour for action.
@export By when must a practitioner whose professional indemnity insurance has lapsed tell their National Board, under the Health Practitioner Regulation National Law (WA)?
GIVEN `the day they became aware` IS A DATE @desc The date the practitioner became aware that appropriate professional indemnity insurance arrangements were no longer in place (ISO 8601)
GIVETH A DATE
DECIDE `By when must the practitioner report lapsed insurance question` IS
    `the s 130 insurance notice deadline from` `the day they became aware`

-- ======================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 237-237B -- my protection as a notifier`

-- s 237(1): "This section applies to a person who, in good faith -- (a) makes a notification under this Law; or (b) gives
-- information in the course of an investigation or for another purpose under this Law to a person exercising functions
-- under this Law." (2): "The person is not liable, civilly, criminally or under an administrative process, for giving the
-- information." (3): "(a) the making of the notification or giving of the information does not constitute a breach of
-- professional etiquette or ethics or a departure from accepted standards of professional conduct; and (b) no liability
-- for defamation is incurred". (4): the protection "extends to -- (a) a person who, in good faith, provided the person with
-- any information on the basis of which the notification was made or the information was given; and (b) a person who,
-- in good faith, was otherwise concerned in the making of the notification or giving of the information."
DECLARE `What I did` IS ONE OF
    `made a notification`
    `gave information in an investigation, or for another purpose under this Law, to a person exercising functions under it`
    `gave the notifier the information the notification was based on`
    `was otherwise concerned in making the notification or giving the information`

DECLARE `Am I protected` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you are not liable civilly, criminally or under an administrative process for it, it is not a breach of professional ethics, and you incur no liability for defamation (s 237(1)-(3))`
    `yes: the protection extends to you (s 237(4)): you are not liable civilly, criminally or under an administrative process, it is not a breach of professional ethics, and you incur no liability for defamation`
    `no: section 237 protects only what is done in good faith`

@export Am I protected from being sued (including for defamation) or punished for making a notification or giving information under the Health Practitioner Regulation National Law (WA)?
GIVEN `what I did` IS A `What I did` @desc What you did: made the notification, gave information to Ahpra or a Board, gave the notifier the information, or were otherwise involved
      `in good faith` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you acted in good faith (honestly, not for an ulterior purpose); a court decides this if it is disputed
GIVETH A `Am I protected`
DECIDE `Am I protected from liability question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`in good faith`)
            THEN `no: section 237 protects only what is done in good faith`
        IF `what I did` EQUALS `made a notification` OR `what I did` EQUALS `gave information in an investigation, or for another purpose under this Law, to a person exercising functions under it`
            THEN `yes: you are not liable civilly, criminally or under an administrative process for it, it is not a breach of professional ethics, and you incur no liability for defamation (s 237(1)-(3))`
        OTHERWISE `yes: the protection extends to you (s 237(4)): you are not liable civilly, criminally or under an administrative process, it is not a breach of professional ethics, and you incur no liability for defamation`

-- s 237A(1): "A person must not -- (a) use threats or intimidation to attempt to persuade another person not to take
-- protected action; or (b) dismiss, or refuse to employ, another person because, or in the belief that, the other person
-- has taken, or intends to take, protected action; or (c) subject another person to other detriment or reprisal because,
-- or in the belief that, the other person has taken, or intends to take, protected action. Maximum penalty -- (a) for an
-- individual -- $60,000; or (b) for a body corporate -- $120,000." (2): "protected action means -- (a) making a
-- notification, in good faith, under this Law; or (b) giving information, documents or other assistance in the course of
-- an investigation or for another purpose under this Law to a person exercising functions under this Law."
DECLARE `My protected action` IS ONE OF
    `making a notification`
    `giving information, documents or other assistance to Ahpra, a Board or an investigator`

DECLARE `What was done to me` IS ONE OF
    `threats or intimidation to persuade me not to notify or help`
    `I was dismissed, or refused employment`
    `some other detriment or reprisal (for example being refused treatment, sued, or demoted)`

DECLARE `Is it a reprisal offence` IS ONE OF
    `yes: using threats or intimidation to try to persuade a person not to take protected action is an offence (s 237A(1)(a))`
    `yes: dismissing or refusing to employ a person because, or in the belief that, they took or intend to take protected action is an offence (s 237A(1)(b))`
    `yes: subjecting a person to other detriment or reprisal because, or in the belief that, they took or intend to take protected action is an offence (s 237A(1)(c))`
    `no: a notification is protected action only if made in good faith (s 237A(2))`
    `no: section 237A(1)(b) and (c) apply only where it was done because, or in the belief that, you took or intended to take protected action`

@export Is it an offence under the Health Practitioner Regulation National Law (WA) for someone to threaten me, sack me or otherwise punish me for notifying Ahpra or helping an investigation?
GIVEN `my action` IS A `My protected action` @desc What you did or intended to do: make a notification, or give information, documents or other help to Ahpra, a Board or an investigator
      `in good faith` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a notification: whether you made (or intend to make) it in good faith
      `what was done` IS A `What was done to me` @desc What the other person did to you
      `because of it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they did it because you had taken, or intended to take, that action (or because they believed so)
      `the day it was done` IS A DATE @desc The date they did it (ISO 8601); s 237A commenced on 1 December 2025
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Is it a reprisal offence`
DECIDE `Is it a reprisal offence question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `the day it was done` `falls before` `the day sections 237A and 237B commenced`
            THEN `not covered` `section 237A commenced on 1 December 2025; earlier conduct falls under the Law as it stood before then, which is not deposited`
        IF `my action` EQUALS `making a notification` AND NOT (`in good faith`)
            THEN `answered` `no: a notification is protected action only if made in good faith (s 237A(2))`
        IF `what was done` EQUALS `threats or intimidation to persuade me not to notify or help`
            THEN `answered` `yes: using threats or intimidation to try to persuade a person not to take protected action is an offence (s 237A(1)(a))`
        IF NOT (`because of it`)
            THEN `answered` `no: section 237A(1)(b) and (c) apply only where it was done because, or in the belief that, you took or intended to take protected action`
        IF `what was done` EQUALS `I was dismissed, or refused employment`
            THEN `answered` `yes: dismissing or refusing to employ a person because, or in the belief that, they took or intend to take protected action is an offence (s 237A(1)(b))`
        OTHERWISE `answered` `yes: subjecting a person to other detriment or reprisal because, or in the belief that, they took or intend to take protected action is an offence (s 237A(1)(c))`

DECLARE `Who did it` IS ONE OF
    `an individual`
    `a body corporate (for example a hospital company or a practice company)`

-- The maximum penalties of s 237A(1).
@export What is the most a person can be fined for a reprisal against a notifier under section 237A of the Health Practitioner Regulation National Law (WA)?
GIVEN `who` IS A `Who did it` @desc Whether the person who did it is an individual or a body corporate
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the maximum reprisal penalty question` IS
    IF `who` EQUALS `an individual` THEN 60000 ELSE 120000

-- s 237B(1): "A provision of a non-disclosure agreement is void to the extent it prevents or limits a person from -- (a)
-- making a notification, in good faith, under this Law; or (b) giving information, documents or other assistance in the
-- course of an investigation or for another purpose under this Law to a person exercising functions under this Law."
-- (3): "non-disclosure agreement means a contract or other agreement that prohibits or restricts the disclosure of
-- information or documents by a person in relation to the health, conduct or performance of a registered health
-- practitioner or former registered health practitioner."
-- s 329: "Section 237B(1) applies -- (a) to a non-disclosure agreement ... whether entered into before or after the
-- commencement of this section; and (b) ... whether the relevant issue occurred before or after the commencement". So the
-- question does not ask when the agreement was made or when the conduct happened.
DECLARE `Does the clause stop me` IS ONE OF
    `no: the clause is void to the extent it prevents or limits you from making a notification in good faith (s 237B(1)(a))`
    `no: the clause is void to the extent it prevents or limits you from giving information, documents or other assistance to a person exercising functions under this Law (s 237B(1)(b))`
    `section 237B does not make it void: the agreement is not a non-disclosure agreement as s 237B(3) defines it (it must restrict disclosure about the health, conduct or performance of a registered or former registered health practitioner)`
    `section 237B does not make it void for a notification not made in good faith (s 237B(1)(a))`

@export Can a confidentiality clause in my settlement or other agreement stop me notifying Ahpra, or helping an investigation, about a health practitioner under the Health Practitioner Regulation National Law (WA)?
GIVEN `it restricts disclosure about a practitioner` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the agreement prohibits or restricts you disclosing information or documents about the health, conduct or performance of a registered (or formerly registered) health practitioner
      `my action` IS A `My protected action` @desc What you want to do: make a notification, or give information, documents or other help to Ahpra, a Board or an investigator
      `in good faith` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a notification: whether you would make it in good faith
GIVETH A `Does the clause stop me`
DECIDE `Can my agreement stop me notifying question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`it restricts disclosure about a practitioner`)
            THEN `section 237B does not make it void: the agreement is not a non-disclosure agreement as s 237B(3) defines it (it must restrict disclosure about the health, conduct or performance of a registered or former registered health practitioner)`
        IF `my action` EQUALS `giving information, documents or other assistance to Ahpra, a Board or an investigator`
            THEN `no: the clause is void to the extent it prevents or limits you from giving information, documents or other assistance to a person exercising functions under this Law (s 237B(1)(b))`
        IF `in good faith`
            THEN `no: the clause is void to the extent it prevents or limits you from making a notification in good faith (s 237B(1)(a))`
        OTHERWISE `section 237B does not make it void for a notification not made in good faith (s 237B(1)(a))`

-- s 237B(2): "A relevant person must not enter into a non-disclosure agreement unless the agreement sets out, clearly and
-- in writing, that the agreement does not limit a person from -- (a) making a notification, in good faith, under this
-- Law; or (b) giving information ... Maximum penalty -- (a) for an individual -- $5,000; or (b) for a body corporate --
-- $10,000." (3): "relevant person means -- (a) an employer or former employer of a registered health practitioner or a
-- person who was registered under this Law; (b) a health service provider whose services are provided by registered
-- health practitioners; (c) a registered health practitioner."
DECLARE `The other party` IS ONE OF
    `the practitioner, or another registered health practitioner`
    `an employer or former employer of a registered or formerly registered health practitioner`
    `a health service provider whose services are provided by registered health practitioners (for example a hospital or clinic)`
    `none of these (for example only an insurer, or a person who is not a practitioner)`

DECLARE `Did they offend` IS ONE OF
    `yes: a relevant person must not enter into a non-disclosure agreement that does not clearly set out in writing that it does not limit notifications or giving information (s 237B(2)); the most it can be fined is shown`
    `no: the agreement clearly sets out in writing that it does not limit notifications or giving information, as s 237B(2) requires`
    `no: section 237B(2) binds only a relevant person (s 237B(3)): a practitioner, a practitioner's employer or former employer, or a health service provider using registered practitioners`
    `no: the agreement is not a non-disclosure agreement as s 237B(3) defines it`
    `no: section 237B(2) commenced on 1 December 2025; it does not reach an agreement entered into before then`

DECLARE `NDA offence answer` HAS
    `the offence answer`       IS A `Did they offend`
    `the most it can be fined` IS A NUMBER

@export Did the other party commit an offence under section 237B of the Health Practitioner Regulation National Law (WA) by making me sign a confidentiality (non-disclosure) agreement, and what is the most they can be fined?
GIVEN `it restricts disclosure about a practitioner` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the agreement prohibits or restricts you disclosing information or documents about the health, conduct or performance of a registered (or formerly registered) health practitioner
      `the other party` IS A `The other party` @desc Who the other party to the agreement is (if several, choose the first that applies in the list)
      `who` IS A `Who did it` @desc Whether that party is an individual or a body corporate
      `it has the carve-out` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the agreement sets out clearly and in writing that it does not limit you notifying Ahpra in good faith or giving information or help to Ahpra, a Board or an investigator
      `the day it was entered into` IS A DATE @desc The date the agreement was made (ISO 8601)
GIVETH AN `NDA offence answer`
DECIDE `Did they commit the NDA offence question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`it restricts disclosure about a practitioner`)
            THEN `NDA offence answer` WITH `the offence answer` IS `no: the agreement is not a non-disclosure agreement as s 237B(3) defines it`, `the most it can be fined` IS 0
        IF `the other party` EQUALS `none of these (for example only an insurer, or a person who is not a practitioner)`
            THEN `NDA offence answer` WITH `the offence answer` IS `no: section 237B(2) binds only a relevant person (s 237B(3)): a practitioner, a practitioner's employer or former employer, or a health service provider using registered practitioners`, `the most it can be fined` IS 0
        IF `it has the carve-out`
            THEN `NDA offence answer` WITH `the offence answer` IS `no: the agreement clearly sets out in writing that it does not limit notifications or giving information, as s 237B(2) requires`, `the most it can be fined` IS 0
        IF `the day it was entered into` `falls before` `the day sections 237A and 237B commenced`
            THEN `NDA offence answer` WITH `the offence answer` IS `no: section 237B(2) commenced on 1 December 2025; it does not reach an agreement entered into before then`, `the most it can be fined` IS 0
        IF `who` EQUALS `an individual`
            THEN `NDA offence answer` WITH `the offence answer` IS `yes: a relevant person must not enter into a non-disclosure agreement that does not clearly set out in writing that it does not limit notifications or giving information (s 237B(2)); the most it can be fined is shown`, `the most it can be fined` IS 5000
        OTHERWISE `NDA offence answer` WITH `the offence answer` IS `yes: a relevant person must not enter into a non-disclosure agreement that does not clearly set out in writing that it does not limit notifications or giving information (s 237B(2)); the most it can be fined is shown`, `the most it can be fined` IS 10000

-- ======================================================================================================================
§§ `Regulative rules -- the deadlines the Law sets`

-- The three periods the Law states in days, each written once here and used by both the seat questions and the
-- regulative rules below. Each is reckoned by Schedule 7 cl 31(2), (3) (see hpnl-dates.l4).
-- s 149(1): "within 60 days after receipt of a notification".
GIVEN received IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the s 149 assessment deadline for a notification received on` received MEANS `the last day of` 60 `days after` received

-- s 192(2)(b): "within 30 days after" the panel's decision.
GIVEN decided IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the s 192 notice deadline for a panel decision on` decided MEANS `the last day of` 30 `days after` decided

-- s 130(1): "within 7 days after becoming aware that a relevant event has occurred".
GIVEN aware IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the s 130 insurance notice deadline from` aware MEANS `the last day of` 7 `days after` aware

-- The parties and actions of the three deadlines the Law states in days. Time is counted in days from the start of each
-- period; the seat questions above give the dates, these rules give the duties.
DECLARE `National Law party` IS ONE OF
    `the National Board`
    `the registered health practitioner`

DECLARE `National Law action` IS ONE OF
    `conduct the preliminary assessment of the notification (s 149(1))`
    `give the notifier written notice of the panel's decision (s 192(2)(b))`
    `give the National Board written notice that indemnity insurance is no longer in place (s 130(1))`

-- s 149(1): the Board MUST conduct the preliminary assessment within 60 days after receipt (extended past a weekend by
-- Schedule 7 cl 31(3)). The Law states no consequence of a late assessment; the LEST records the breach only.
GIVEN received IS A DATE
GIVETH A DEONTIC `National Law party` `National Law action`
`the s 149 assessment duty for a notification received on` received MEANS
    PARTY `the National Board`
    MUST  `conduct the preliminary assessment of the notification (s 149(1))`
    WITHIN `days from` received `to` (`the s 149 assessment deadline for a notification received on` received)
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST  BREACH BY `the National Board` BECAUSE "s 149(1): no preliminary assessment within 60 days after receipt"

-- s 192(2)(b): the Board MUST give the notifier written notice of the panel's decision within 30 days after it is made.
GIVEN decided IS A DATE
GIVETH A DEONTIC `National Law party` `National Law action`
`the s 192 notice duty for a panel decision on` decided MEANS
    PARTY `the National Board`
    MUST  `give the notifier written notice of the panel's decision (s 192(2)(b))`
    WITHIN `days from` decided `to` (`the s 192 notice deadline for a panel decision on` decided)
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST  BREACH BY `the National Board` BECAUSE "s 192(2)(b): the notifier was not told of the panel's decision within 30 days"

-- s 130(1), (3)(c): the practitioner MUST notify the Board within 7 days after becoming aware the insurance has lapsed.
-- s 130(2): not an offence, but may be behaviour for which health, conduct or performance action may be taken.
GIVEN aware IS A DATE
GIVETH A DEONTIC `National Law party` `National Law action`
`the s 130 insurance notice duty from` aware MEANS
    PARTY `the registered health practitioner`
    MUST  `give the National Board written notice that indemnity insurance is no longer in place (s 130(1))`
    WITHIN `days from` aware `to` (`the s 130 insurance notice deadline from` aware)
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST  BREACH BY `the registered health practitioner` BECAUSE "s 130(1): no notice to the Board within 7 days of becoming aware the indemnity insurance had lapsed (not an offence: s 130(2))"
